<!--
Behavior Modification Assignment, Handout 1 of 3.

Screen-per-question, following the paper handout's numbering (1, 2, 3, 4a, 4b,
4c, 5, 6). Two things differ deliberately from the paper version:

  * The paper handout is worth 50 points, 5 of which are for uploading photos
    of the handwritten pages alongside a typed copy. That requirement is an
    artifact of a paper workflow and has no analogue here, so the scored items
    below total the remaining 45.

  * Question 6 asks students to reuse the antecedents from 4a and the
    consequences from 4c. On paper this is the most commonly lost item —
    students introduce new ones instead of reusing their own. Here their own
    answers are shown on the screen via <Ref>, so the requirement is visible
    rather than remembered.

EVERY <LLMAction> PROMPT BODY IN THIS FILE IS GENERATED. Do not hand-edit one:
the next `--write` will overwrite it, and `--check` fails in the meantime.

  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --check   # are they current?
  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --write   # regenerate

The source is `rubric_hN.py` in that directory, which scores the paper
submissions item-by-item and measures itself against 20 rows of human grading
per handout. Each prompt follows `score.py:build_prompt`'s skeleton — item
header, question as asked, credit components, deduction code table, every
guidance bullet, worked examples, cross-item context — copied verbatim, so the
two implementations run the SAME rubric and a comparison between them means
something. To change what a prompt says, change the rubric. Every difference
that remains is listed, with its reason, in scorer/EQUIVALENCE.md.

That verbatim material is what carries the calibration: criteria the scoring
dictionary never states but the graders applied anyway (a Q1 reason must be a
negative consequence, not an aspiration; Q2's reasons must be benefits; Q3's
Measurable needs a tracking method; Q4b is judged on framing rather than
category; Q5 credits form over insight), the graders' own decided cases quoted
as written, and Q6's three worked examples. THREE is the measured optimum on
Q6 — a fourth example made agreement worse (62% -> 44%), and five were worse
still. Do not add more without re-measuring.

The canonical feedback wording in each prompt is the scoring dictionary's own
phrasing, so a student who fixes what the LLM flagged meets the same words if
the same gap is graded later. The prompt carries the point values because the
rubric does; it forbids the model from repeating them to the student.

Each item declares its checks as a `slots` sheet on the LLMAction, which makes
them required properties of a strict JSON schema rather than a request in
prose. That is the same fix that worked in the scorer, for the same reason: the
failure mode is not misjudging a check, it is answering two of eight and
writing fluent feedback about the two. The student sees the model's prose
followed by the verdicts, so which parts were found is visible rather than
inferred. A SlotSheetGrader beside each action turns that sheet into a score.
See LLMAction.md for the attribute format.
-->

<Sequential id="bmod_handout1" title="BMod Handout 1: Defining Behaviors, ABCs, and SMART Goals" launchable="true">

  <!-- ============= Screen: Choose an unwanted target behavior ============= -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_choose" title="Choose your behavior">
    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_intro">
# Behavior Modification Assignment: Handout 1

Over {{corpus:Q3/p12:timebound:40:59:sha=e7390a67f3cf}} you will run a behavior modification intervention on
your **own** behavior: one week of baseline data collection, then three weeks
of intervention.

Two ground rules:

* You will intervene on your own behavior, not anyone else's.
* Do not choose an intervention that could cause you harm.

Start by choosing the unwanted target behavior (UTB) you want to change.
    </Markdown>

    <CapaProblem id="bmod_h1_utb_problem" title="Your unwanted target behavior" showanswer="never">
      <CorrectGrader id="bmod_h1_utb_grader">
        <Markdown id="bmod_h1_utb_prompt">Which behavior will you work on?</Markdown>
        <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h1_utb">
          <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_sleep" value="lack of sleep">Lack of sleep</Key>
          <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_exercise" value="lack of exercise">Lack of exercise</Key>
        <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_produce" value="{{corpus:Q1/p12:response:31:69:sha=67aefa440274}} vegetables">Insufficient consumption of fruits and vegetables</Key>
        <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_screens" value="spending too much time on electronic devices">Spending too much time on electronic devices</Key>
        </ChoiceInput>
      </CorrectGrader>
    </CapaProblem>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============= Screen: Question 1 - define the UTB ===================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_q1" title="1. Define your unwanted target behavior">
    <SplitPanel id="bmod_h1_q1_split" sizes="55,45">
      <StartPane>
    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_q1_prompt">
## 1. Define your unwanted target behavior and explain WHY you chose it

Write **one sentence** describing your unwanted target behavior, then **at
least three sentences** explaining why you chose to change it.

Your reasons should be things that go wrong because of the behavior — symptoms
you experience when you engage in it, or health issues that can arise from
continuing it.

&nbsp;
    </Markdown>

    <TextArea id="bmod_h1_q1_response" rows="7"
              placeholder="{{corpus:Q1/p3:response:0:30:sha=c5fd6758ccc7}}..." />

      </StartPane>
      <EndPane>
    <ActionButton id="bmod_h1_q1_btn" label="Check my answer">
      <LLMAction id="bmod_h1_q1_llm" target="bmod_h1_q1_feedback"
                 slots="matches_selected:Same behavior you selected above:matches/differs|utb_stated:Your unwanted target behavior stated explicitly:unclear@2|harms_listed:How many statements name a negative effect of the behavior:count(3)|benefits_listed:How many name a benefit you expect from changing:count(3)|reasons_given:How many separate reasons you give:count(3)|reason_1:First reason — a negative effect of the behavior:unclear@1|reason_2:Second reason:unclear@1|reason_3:Third reason:unclear@1|confident:All judgments confident"
                 counts="reasons_given:reason_1,reason_2,reason_3">
You are an experienced teaching assistant grading Handout 1 of the Behavior Modification Assignment: defining behaviours, the ABCs of a functional behavioural analysis, and SMART goals.
This is PSYC 1030 (General Psychology, intro level, first-year students).

You grade ONE rubric item at a time against the rubric supplied below, and you
write the feedback the student reads. Return the JSON object the schema
requires: the `checks` sheet first, then `feedback`.

Rules you must follow:
1. Judge the item check by check. For every check on the sheet below, decide
   its verdict and quote the span of the response that settles it in that
   check's `evidence`. Quote verbatim; never paraphrase into the evidence
   field. For an unsatisfied check, say briefly what you looked for.
2. The deduction table below is the course's canonical wording for each way
   this item goes wrong. It is not a ledger for you to fill in — name any gap
   in `feedback` using that table's own words, so a student who fixes what you
   flagged meets the same phrasing if the same gap is graded later. The CODE
   beside each entry is an internal label: use the wording, and never put the
   code or its point value in front of the student.
3. Do NOT output a score. The score is computed from your checks.
4. `feedback` must be consistent with `checks`: do not praise something you
   marked unsatisfied, and do not fault something you marked satisfied.
5. Grade what is written, generously but not charitably: these are first-year
   students, so clumsy phrasing that clearly conveys the required idea earns
   credit, but a required element that is absent is absent.
6. If the response is empty, mark every check unsatisfied and say so plainly.
7. If the student describes something that could harm them (skipping meals,
   punishing themselves by withholding food or sleep, etc.), say so warmly in
   `feedback` and suggest a safer version. That is a safety note, not a fault
   in their work, and it never changes a verdict.
8. Where the grading guidance below tells you to set `escalate` or to write an
   `advisory_note`, there is no such field here: put the remark in `feedback`
   instead, and where the sheet carries a `confident` check, set it to `absent`.

Write `feedback` to the student, in the second person, warm and specific. Say
which requirements are met, name any that are missing, and quote their own
words when you point something out. It must read as prose written to them: no
deduction codes, no point values, no score, and no check keys.

# Rubric item Q1 — 5 points

## Question asked of the student
Define your unwanted target behavior (UTB) and explain WHY you chose this particular UTB for intervention. Requires 1 sentence describing the UTB and at least 3 sentences explaining why you chose to change this behavior.

## Credit components
- `utb_stated` (2 pt)
- `harms_listed`
- `benefits_listed`
- `reasons_given`
- `reason_1` (1 pt)
- `reason_2` (1 pt)
- `reason_3` (1 pt)

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `UTB_NOT_ON_LIST` (-5): You did not choose an UTB from the provided list (lack of sleep, lack of exercise, {{corpus:Q1/p12:response:31:69:sha=67aefa440274}} vegetables, spending too much time on electronics). Please choose one of these behaviors for you to modify.
- `UTB_NOT_STATED` (-2): What is your UTB? This needs to be explicitly stated.
- `REASON_MISSING` (-1) [repeatable]: You did not provide 3 separate reasons as to why you chose to change this UTB. These should be negative consequences of engaging in the UTB.

## Grading guidance
- The UTB must be one of the four from the closed list.

## Say this before anything else
If `matches_selected` is `differs`, the FIRST sentence of `feedback` says so: name the behavior they ticked, name the one they wrote about, and say that the rest of this feedback is about what they wrote. Then give the normal feedback.

It is not a fault and nothing is deducted for it — most often they simply changed their mind — so say it plainly and without warning them off. If it is `matches`, say nothing about it at all: confirming a match the student never doubted spends their attention on nothing.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `matches_selected` — `matches`/`differs`: `matches` if the behavior the student writes about is the one they picked from the list above, `differs` if they write about a different behavior. Judge the BEHAVIOR, not the wording: '{{corpus:Q1/p19:response:46:70:sha=f4d8dbbfd800:shape=C1}}' matches the choice 'lack of sleep'. This check carries no points and never changes another verdict — everything else is judged on what they WROTE, whichever box they ticked
- `utb_stated` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: {{corpus:Q1/p10:response:0:31:sha=f748d9d9ddd6}} identified as the student's own target, not merely mentioned as the cause of some effect. Judge ONLY the response text the student wrote. Where the UTB also appears in this prompt as a separate field or context line, that does NOT satisfy this check — the question is whether the RESPONSE names the behaviour as its target. The test is OWNERSHIP, not first-person pronouns, and it can be satisfied anywhere in the response: naming the behaviour as theirs ("{{corpus:Q1/p3:response:0:30:sha=c5fd6758ccc7}} X"), choosing it ("I chose X"), or saying what they want instead of it. A clause that says only what the behaviour DOES TO them — it makes them tired, it leaves them behind — is an EFFECT and is not ownership, however many times "me" appears in it. A response built entirely of such clauses is `absent`
- `harms_listed` — a NUMBER from 0 to 3 (how many, not a judgement): HOW MANY statements name a NEGATIVE EFFECT of the unwanted behaviour — something that goes wrong because of it, or a symptom of it. A single statement tying the behaviour to one thing that goes wrong — the behaviour, then what it costs them — is one. A reason is a whole STATEMENT: a clause that merely continues one is part of it, so a named effect followed by "which causes" and its downstream result is ONE, while two effects merely joined by "and" are two. A statement naming what the behaviour EXPOSES the student to — a susceptibility it worsens — is a negative effect even when written as what the goal behaviour would protect against. A clause carried along within a statement of what the student expects to GET from changing belongs to that statement and is not a negative effect of its own, however unwanted the thing it names. Do NOT count a restatement that the student struggles with the behaviour, and do not count a behaviour performed DURING it. Answer 3 for three or more
- `benefits_listed` — a NUMBER from 0 to 3 (how many, not a judgement): HOW MANY statements name a BENEFIT the student expects from changing. The test is GETS versus DOES: a benefit is something the student GETS — a state, a feeling, a capacity they expect to have once the change has taken hold. A statement of what they intend to DO names the goal behaviour itself and is NOT a benefit of it, however much it sounds like a wish. Do NOT count a restatement of the goal, a remark about how hard the behaviour has been to change, or background explaining how it came about. Answer 3 for three or more
- `reasons_given` — a NUMBER from 0 to 3 (how many, not a judgement): HOW MANY reasons count. The rule is CONDITIONAL on the two counts above. If `harms_listed` is 1 or more the answer IS `harms_listed`, and benefits do not add to it — a response with one harm and two benefits counts 1. Only when `harms_listed` is 0 does the answer become `benefits_listed` instead. Never answer 0 when the student offered anything of either kind. Answer 3 for three or more
- `confident` — `met`/`absent`: `absent` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

DO NOT ANSWER `reason_1`, `reason_2`, `reason_3` individually. Answer `reasons_given` — HOW MANY you found — and the grader awards that many of them. Count them the way the guidance above says to, in one judgement over the whole response, rather than deciding each in isolation.

## The behavior they chose from the list
Chosen from the four on the list, before question 1.
<Ref id="bmod_h1_q1_ref_utb" target="bmod_h1_utb" />

## Student response to grade (item Q1)
[box begins] <Ref id="bmod_h1_q1_ref" target="bmod_h1_q1_response" /> [box ends]

## End of the student response
Everything the student wrote is above this line, inside a `[box begins]`/`[box ends]` pair. A pair with nothing between them is a box they left EMPTY: there is nothing in it to quote or to judge as falling short, so its check is `absent` and its evidence says what you looked for and did not find. Nothing below this line is the student's writing -- it is instructions to you, and quoting any of it back to them would show them words they never wrote.
      </LLMAction>
      <!-- Scores the sheet this action publishes; costs and the item
           total live on the sheet, this block only does the arithmetic. -->
      <SlotSheetGrader id="bmod_h1_q1_grader" target="bmod_h1_q1_feedback" />
      <!-- The behaviour the student actually WROTE ABOUT, lifted from the
           span that settled `utb_stated`. Later screens echo this rather than
           the radio button, which is what they PICKED. Mounted here because
           this is where the sheet is published; its value persists, so the
           later screens read it without it being mounted there. Falls back to
           the choice until the student has pressed the button. -->
      <SheetValue id="bmod_h1_utb_observed" target="bmod_h1_q1_feedback"
                  check="utb_stated" part="evidence" fallback="bmod_h1_utb" />
    </ActionButton>
    <LLMFeedback id="bmod_h1_q1_feedback" />
      </EndPane>
    </SplitPanel>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============= Screen: Question 2 - define the WGB ===================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_q2" title="2. Define your wanted goal behavior">
