# Rubric item Q3 — 5 points

## Question asked of the student
Use the SMART goal acronym to further define your WGB. Each aspect must be discussed AND labeled: Specific, Measurable, Actionable/Action-Oriented, Realistic, Time-Bound.

## Credit components
- `specific` (1 pt): Names a specific target, quantified
- `measurable` (1 pt): Says how it will be measured
- `action_oriented` (1 pt): Names something the student will DO, or a concrete circumstance that lets them do it — an activity — a step they will physically carry out, at a stated time or not — or access to a PLACE or EQUIPMENT that lets them do it. Be generous about the doing: a plain statement of what they will physically perform counts, and so does naming somewhere they can go or something they can use. TIME ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, and this is where the graders drew the line: hours they could give to it, or expect to clear, name neither a doing nor any means of doing it. An answer that leans entirely on how much of the week it could occupy loses this point; an answer naming somewhere to go or something to use keeps it, whatever it says about hours. A bare capability with no activity and no place or equipment named fails for the same reason. What FAILS is a response that justifies actionability by pointing at another letter of SMART instead of at any doing — and names no doing of its own, or that only re-labels the goal as actionable without saying what is done
- `realistic` (1 pt): Is realistic, or says why it is
- `time_bound` (1 pt): FOUR WEEKS TOTAL is the test. One week of baseline {{corpus:Q3/p13:timebound:119:144:sha=b4ef1f64f7b0}} is how most students phrase it, and any wording that comes to four weeks satisfies this check — the two halves in either order, or a bare "four weeks" with no split named. A duration that does not total four weeks loses the point, whatever else it says

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `SPECIFIC_NOT_QUANTIFIED` (-1): -1 pt: For specific, you should have a specific, quantifiable goal. (ex. 30 minutes a day).
- `MEASURABLE_NO_METHOD` (-1): -1 pt: For measurable, how are you tracking your goal? (ex. in a notebook).
- `ACTION_MISSING` (-1): -1 pt: For action, what do you have to actively do to achieve your goal?
- `REALISTIC_MISSING` (-1): -1 pt: missing realistic.
- `TIME_BOUND_WRONG` (-1): -1 pt: For time-bound, you have one week of baseline data and three weeks of intervention, or 4 weeks total.

## Grading guidance
- Award each of the five letters independently, 1 point each.
- Time-Bound has a near-verbatim expected answer: four weeks total, one week of baseline data collection and three weeks of intervention data collection. Any other duration loses the point.
- IMPLICIT (from gold): Measurable must name a tracking METHOD or LOCATION (a notebook, a phone app). Restating the goal is not measuring it. Specific must carry a NUMBER.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `specific` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: Names a specific target, quantified
- `measurable` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: Says how it will be measured
- `action_oriented` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: Names something the student will DO, or a concrete circumstance that lets them do it — an activity — a step they will physically carry out, at a stated time or not — or access to a PLACE or EQUIPMENT that lets them do it. Be generous about the doing: a plain statement of what they will physically perform counts, and so does naming somewhere they can go or something they can use. TIME ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, and this is where the graders drew the line: hours they could give to it, or expect to clear, name neither a doing nor any means of doing it. An answer that leans entirely on how much of the week it could occupy loses this point; an answer naming somewhere to go or something to use keeps it, whatever it says about hours. A bare capability with no activity and no place or equipment named fails for the same reason. What FAILS is a response that justifies actionability by pointing at another letter of SMART instead of at any doing — and names no doing of its own, or that only re-labels the goal as actionable without saying what is done
- `realistic` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: Is realistic, or says why it is
- `time_bound` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: FOUR WEEKS TOTAL is the test. One week of baseline {{corpus:Q3/p13:timebound:119:144:sha=b4ef1f64f7b0}} is how most students phrase it, and any wording that comes to four weeks satisfies this check — the two halves in either order, or a bare "four weeks" with no split named. A duration that does not total four weeks loses the point, whatever else it says
- `confident` — `met`/`absent`: `absent` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

